Start();
include(unitTests);
include(utilities);
search reset stack Grokking UnitTests Utilities list;

register(TraitFlagsT);
define
    tfSeen; tfAttrs; tfTraits; tfDisagree; tfBoth; tfNeither;
    tfProbe code={
        tfA := hasAttributeS;
        tfB := hasTraitS;
        tfMix = 0;
        if tfA;             tfMix = 1;
        if tfB;             tfMix = tfMix + 1;
        if tfMix == 0;      ++tfNeither;
        if tfMix == 1;
            ++tfDisagree;
            tfDisagree  +% argument;
        if tfMix == 2;      ++tfBoth;
        if tfB;             ++tfTraits;
        if tfA;             ++tfAttrs;
        ++tfSeen;
        };
    tfWalk code={
        iterate tfCur on *argument;
        while ++tfCur;
            tfProbe(*tfCur);
        };
    tfTagLine code={
        print `taG;
        iterate grup on argument;
        while ++grup;
            print _$taG;
            if datA;    print $"=" grup;
        print :;
        };
    tfRun code={
        tfSeen = 0;  tfAttrs = 0;  tfTraits = 0;  tfDisagree = 0;  tfBoth = 0;  tfNeither = 0;
        tfWalk(Grokking);
        cerr "TF-1 rules seen                   = " tfSeen:;
        cerr "TF-2 carrying hasAttributeS       = " tfAttrs:;
        cerr "TF-3 carrying hasTraitS           = " tfTraits:;
        cerr "TF-4 carrying BOTH                = " tfBoth:;
        cerr "TF-6 no attributes                = " tfNeither:;
        cerr "TF-5 the two flags DISAGREE on    = " tfDisagree " rules":;
        print "TF-7 the disagreeing rules:":;
        iterate grup on tfDisagree;
        while ++grup;
            tfTagLine(*grup);
        };
    ;
tfRun();
print "TRAITFLAGS SENTINEL -- reached the foot":;
stop();

traitFlagsT -- hasAttributeS AND hasTraitS OVER THE RAW GRAMMAR, AND THE FIVE RULES
THEY DISAGREE ABOUT. Promoted into the fleet 2026-09-15 from incant/fixits/hasTraits,
which retires to incant/attic on these rows.

⚠⚠ TF-5 READS 2, AND THE TraiT PACKET QUESTION IS CLOSED. Tony ruled the transport packet
off aCTionTraiT on 2026-09-15, the day this fixture was minted. ANYstring, leftCurly and
rightCurly carried that packet as their ONLY attribute, so removing it leaves them with no
attributes at all and both flags read 0 -- agreement. TF-2 went 52 -> 49 and TF-6 34 -> 37.

⚠⚠ THE TWO SURVIVORS ARE A DIFFERENT CAUSE, AND THEY WERE NAMED BEFORE THE BUILD RATHER
THAN EXPLAINED AFTER IT. ShortcuT and StatemenT disagree because their only ATTRIBUTE is
`builtinActoR`, which setActionMethod publishes noPrint on every rule carrying a dlsym-able
action. A rule whose attributes are all noPrint reads hasAttributeS 1 / hasTraitS 0 whatever
the packet does. Measured per attribute, with affiliation, before the removal was built:

    ShortcuT    builtinActoR/nP=1/attr=1
    StatemenT   builtinActoR/nP=1/attr=1, then SemI BlocK WardeD Iterate Xpress
                -- all attr=0, MEMBERS, and a member cannot carry a trait

⚠ SO ShortcuT CARRIED THE PACKET AND NEVER DISAGREED BECAUSE OF IT. It had two noPrint
attributes and now has one; the flags could not move. Four rules carried the packet and only
THREE ever disagreed because of it -- a distinction the 09-10 write-up did not draw, and the
reason a certificate of "TF-5 -> 0" could not have been met.

⚠ TF-5 MOVING IS STILL NEWS EITHER WAY. Down to 0 means something took builtinActoR off, or
hasTraits learned to ignore it. Up means a new population joined. Do not re-pin it without a
sentence saying which.

⚠ TF-4 IS WHAT STOPS TF-5 BEING VACUOUS, and it is not decoration. TF-5 wants a count,
and a run whose probe read nothing at all would print a count too -- zero, or whatever
a broken walk happened to reach. TF-4 at 47 is a non-zero sibling on the same walk, so
the pair distinguishes "they disagree on two" from "nothing was measured". Rule H4's
anti-vacuity clause, and the reason every zero-expecting row here has one.

⚠ TF-7 PRINTS THE NAMES, and that is the row that survives a renumbering. A count tells
you something moved; the names tell you WHAT, without anybody re-deriving the walk. It is
presence-with-value applied to a population instead of to a quantity.

⚠ WHAT WAS DROPPED IN THE PROMOTION, deliberately, and it is not coverage lost. The
retired citizen's HP-0..HP-5 rows attempted the same census AFTER setParse and came back
VACUOUS: setParse TOOK on three members out of sixty-four, because a bare master lawfully
has no rStuff (Ruling D) and refuses by name. A member-walk of Grokking cannot prime this
population, so HP-5's zero was a reading of a population that was never primed. The
post-setParse divergence IS certified, with a hit control, by connectiveT's two fleet rows
-- StatemenT reads 1/0 after setParse, BlocK's hit control still reads 1/1. Agreement
before and divergence after are now pinned from both sides, one instrument each.

⚠ THE WALK IS OVER Grokking ITSELF AND NOT OVER ITS MEMBERS, and that word cost a census
once. `iterate ... members on` skips the 22 rules that sit in Grokking's ATTRIBUTE list --
ANYorNum, ANYstring, NamE, StatemenT, Token and eighteen others -- so ShortcuT's exact
twin never appeared in the disagreement list and the population silently excluded a known
member of itself (rule H11). Dropping the word takes TF-1 from 64 to 86.

⚠ TWO SPELLING TRAPS INHERITED WITH THE FILE, both still live.
  . Passing a rule BY NAME crashes the run at exit 139 with zero bytes: naming a rule
    FIRES its action (bear-trap 34), and a block-executing action does exactly that. The
    walk over Grokking is not a stylistic choice.
  . `if tfA != tfB;` counts 66 disagreements while TF-2 and TF-3 read 42 -- arithmetically
    impossible, and `!=` on two captured flag locals is void. `if tfA == 1;` reads 0 for
    all of them, because a flag's stored value is a bitfield and not literally 1. The
    working shape is BARE for the flag, letting truthOf answer it, and `==` only on a
    derived counter.
